WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.380
CHAITALI NARLA: Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.650
Our topic of the day
is Mindful Testing--

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.450
Balancing Test Coverage
and Maintenance.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:19.940
Before we dive into the topic,
a quick introduction to us.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:23.320
My name is Chaitali, and
I'm an engineering manager

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:28.935
in Google Cloud's Engineering
Productivity Organization.

00:00:28.935 --> 00:00:30.215
SHADI KHALEK: Hello, everyone.

00:00:30.215 --> 00:00:31.350
My name is Shadi.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:34.140
I'm an engineering manager
in Google Cloud's Engineering

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.850
Productivity Organization, too.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.830
CHAITALI NARLA: So today,
we'll be talking to you

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:44.200
about what is mindful testing.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.660
We also would like
to talk to you

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.980
about why mindful testing is
an important concept that you

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.380
could practice in
your organization,

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.200
and what benefit
that would give you.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.690
We will talk about
five tips on how you

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.300
can practice mindful testing.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.270
Along the way, we'll
talk about various tools

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:07.800
and processes we use at Google
and especially in Google Cloud

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.100
to apply mindful
testing to our products.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.660
So reading from the definition
in the Oxford Dictionary,

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.810
mindfulness is the
quality or state

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:22.800
of being conscious or
aware of something.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.500
So what is mindful testing?

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.070
To understand this, let's
talk about integration testing

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:33.990
to which the concept of mindful
testing applies the most.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:37.960
We know that integration
tests are a complex system.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:44.300
We know that we have to
constantly balance trade-offs

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.660
between too many tests
and too few tests when

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:50.150
it comes to integration tests.

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.730
If you have too many
integration tests,

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:55.680
you may have great
coverage of your product,

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.780
but your release
lifecycle might be slowed.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:03.230
If you have too few tests, you
may be releasing very quickly,

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:07.025
but at the cost possibly
of reliability and velocity

00:02:07.025 --> 00:02:07.650
of your system.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:13.270
We also know that
tests have a lifecycle.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:15.370
If you want to
add a new feature,

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:17.350
you may need to add new tests.

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:19.652
If you change or
deprecate a feature,

00:02:19.652 --> 00:02:21.610
you may need to change
your deprecate the tests

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:23.570
that you have.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:26.500
So in summary,
what we believe is

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:30.010
tests can help you
improve product quality,

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:33.580
but only through
thoughtful care.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:38.240
So going back to mindful
testing, how do we define that?

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:41.150
We define it as
the art and science

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:45.920
of writing and maintaining
just the right amount of tests

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.070
that give you enough
product coverage.

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:52.550
Why mindful testing?

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:54.440
There are four
reasons that we would

00:02:54.440 --> 00:03:00.270
like to present to you about my
mindful testing is important.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.870
The first one is reliability.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:07.320
You want to find those bugs
before your customers do.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.300
You also want to
make sure that you

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.270
are delivering a stable
and dependable product

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.100
to your customers.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.415
Mindful testing can help
you provide that reliably.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.620
Our second reason
is time to market.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.900
If you want faster
time to market

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.570
for your features
in your product,

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:31.270
mindful testing can
help you achieved that

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:36.690
with a good balance between
too many and too few tests.

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:41.210
The third reason is technical
debt and reducing debt.

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:44.960
And the fourth reason is
developer productivity.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:47.510
Both of these, the
third and fourth reason,

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:51.740
are great reasons to make sure
your engineers are happier,

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:53.640
and who doesn't want that?

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.100
So hopefully,
we've convinced you

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:58.100
that mindful testing is an
interesting concept that

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:02.510
can help you and your
products overall.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:05.630
Let's talk about five
tips for mindful testing.

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:07.130
SHADI KHALEK: We
will present to you

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:09.320
five useful tips
to achieve mindful

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:11.390
testing that we
continuously tried

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:15.260
to adopt for our teams
and our products here.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:18.410
To start with,
listen to your tests.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:22.550
When they fail, they are telling
you something very important.

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:24.440
Now to listen to
your tests and be

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:26.550
able to take action
when they fail,

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.430
you need to start
with a setup that

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:31.960
has high signal-to-noise ratio.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:35.740
At Google, we do this by
ensuring that every failure

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:38.110
type has an owner.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:42.670
And we have routing tools that
automatically route failures

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:45.470
correctly to the
corresponding team.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:49.840
For example, at GCE, integration
end-to-end test failure

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:53.700
could be routed to the control
plane, the virtualization,

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:57.660
the [INAUDIBLE] OS or the
kernel team, and so on.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:02.360
Also, we want to take immediate
action when failures happen.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:05.540
We achieve this by
setting response SLOs

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:07.520
to our test failures.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.000
And we do this by having
bug master rotations

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.700
from different teams to
keep an eye on the builds

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:15.850
when they fail.

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:18.850
We also have a philosophy
of roll back first

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:21.330
and investigate later.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:23.130
We have playbooks
that developers

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:25.740
follow to make their job
easier in investigating

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:28.740
those failures.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:33.030
In addition, we need to
follow up on large scale test

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:35.800
breakages with postmortems.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:39.240
The postmortem analysis
look into how could we

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.150
prevent such breakages
from happening again

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:45.060
and how could we shift
our testing to the left.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:47.820
And also, our
philosophy here is write

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:50.110
the smallest test possible.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:54.370
We try to apply the same
rigor of production outages

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:57.670
to our test outages,
especially the integration

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:00.140
and end-to-end tests.

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:02.500
Help your tests stay healthy.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:06.020
They need to help
developing good habits.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:09.160
The first habit
we try to develop

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:12.580
is running on hermetic
test infrastructure

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:14.880
versus non-hermetic ones.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:18.470
And familiar test
environments allow your tests

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:21.980
to run in isolation, be
repeatable, and easier

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:24.050
to debug.

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:25.520
Here, this is an
example of KIND,

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:30.440
which is Kubernetes-in-Docker
as a system in a box test

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:31.970
infrastructure.

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:34.970
It enables you to
run hermetic tests

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:37.430
and it allows you
to run integration

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:41.230
tests for Kubernetes, Istio,
or Anthos application.

00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:43.340
The environment here
would be isolated

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:48.170
with no external dependencies
and be completely familiar.

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:50.720
We also want to ensure
that tests clean up

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:52.250
after themselves.

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:55.760
Now this is needed mostly when
we share the test environment

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:57.720
across different tests.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:00.620
We know that setting up a
hermetic and familiar test

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:03.890
environment is not possible
for every single test suite,

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:06.440
either because of
the cost or because

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:09.060
of the complexity of systems.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.800
So in those cases, where we
are sharing a test environment

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:13.430
across different
test suites, we need

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:17.360
to make sure that we clean
up every single test run

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:21.380
and we keep the shared
environment in a good state.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:24.650
To enable compute engine tests
to clean up after themselves

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:30.150
effectively, we create the
tools as project rental service.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:35.060
This allows GCE developers
to use fewer test projects

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:37.520
to run their integration tests.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:41.090
As a test starts to run,
they would go and run

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:44.150
the project out of
a pool of projects.

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:47.310
They would use the project
to do any testing needed.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:48.920
And then when they
are done, they

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:51.330
would return the
project to the service.

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:54.800
Now the service is responsible
of cleaning up the project,

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:57.140
deleting all
resources, and setting

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:59.990
the project back to a
clean slate, which could be

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:02.450
used again for other testing.

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:05.480
This separates the test
from the cleanup needed,

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:07.160
and guarantees
that the cleanup is

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:11.120
done effectively and correctly.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:14.890
We also want to invest in root
causes and debugging tools.

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:18.010
No matter what environment we're
running again, either if it's

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:20.710
hermetic or shared
environment, we need good root

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:22.900
causing and debugging tools.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:25.630
For example, tools
like Cloud Debugger

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:28.390
can help you significantly
in debugging your cloud

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:29.980
application.

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:32.559
CHAITALI NARLA: The target
is to discipline your test

00:08:32.559 --> 00:08:38.799
with respect, because sometimes,
they will try your patience.

00:08:38.799 --> 00:08:43.809
Our strategy for having healthy
tests, the cornerstone of this,

00:08:43.809 --> 00:08:46.600
is to manage flakiness.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:50.020
Let's talk a little bit about
why managing flakiness is

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:52.360
an important tool.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:55.300
Consider a microservices
architecture

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:58.390
with about 40
critical components.

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:03.430
If each of those components
is only to 0.26% flaky,

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:09.100
you would still get an overall
environmental flakiness of 10%.

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:13.510
To get an overall environment
flakiness of about 1.5%,

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:17.820
which is a good standard for
being able to release products

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:21.070
quickly, you would still
need each component

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:26.170
to have just 0.38% flakiness.

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:29.390
So hopefully, this shows
you why managing flakiness

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:32.250
is critical for us.

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:35.280
We manage flakiness
with two approaches.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.390
One is that we track flakiness
numbers really closely,

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:43.370
so we know when and where
and how blindness shows up

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:46.600
in our integration tests.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:49.860
Here's an example of a graph
that one of our metrics tools

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:51.240
generated.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:54.450
As you can see here,
it shows a 90 day trend

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:59.440
for not only how much flakiness
is present in our systems,

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:02.070
but also, the number
of test targets

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:05.050
that were affected
by this flakiness.

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:08.590
So this is an example of
this and many other metrics

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:11.620
we track around flakiness
to help us understand

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:14.520
how to combat it better.

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:18.740
We also invest in long
term flakiness pictures.

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:22.530
This is an example of a before
and after from a flakiness

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:23.620
fix it.

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:26.130
As you can see, the
before has a lot

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:29.670
more inconsistency at
flakiness while the after

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:32.400
looks a lot more consistent
in terms of the test

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:34.180
results we see there.

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:37.710
Fixes are a longtime
tradition at Google,

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:41.760
where teams will set aside
time from their regular work

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:44.700
for up to a week
or more sometimes

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:48.400
to focus on engineering
excellence teams.

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:52.570
And flakiness is a very
popular theme to focus on.

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:54.870
So in summary, we
discipline our tests

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:59.370
by managing flakiness, tracking
it, and making sure that we

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:02.580
prioritize long term fixes.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:05.040
SHADI KHALEK: Teach
your tests independence,

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.880
so you don't have to tend
to their needs often.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:09.860
We really want to
lower the maintenance

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:12.410
cost of all our tests.

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:14.610
Maintaining the tests
is very expensive,

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:18.400
so we start with proper
capacity planning.

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:20.650
We need to provide
adequate resources

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:24.730
to run our tests
quickly and correctly.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:28.720
At Google, we do this by taking
inventory of all our test

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:33.310
resources needed once a year
to make sure that the existing

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:36.310
tests could run properly,
and also, to ensure that we

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:38.380
have enough capacity
during the coming

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:41.060
year for additional tests.

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:44.420
The graph here shows you why
capacity planning is important

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:46.760
and measuring is
important, as well.

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:50.660
Here, we could see that there
is an amount of unused resources

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:52.790
for running that single test.

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:55.540
We could reduce the resources
allocated for that test

00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:58.250
so that we could run
either more tests

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:01.730
or we could increase the
parallelism of our testing,

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:04.990
and reduce the time
to finish the tests.

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:08.390
Write-once run-anywhere
is our philosophy

00:12:08.390 --> 00:12:10.760
to get the maximum
out of your tests,

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:13.420
especially when you're
dealing with integration

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:14.960
end-to-end testing.

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:17.780
This allows you to run
tests against different test

00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:20.910
environments and to
validate them quicker.

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:23.540
Anthos this is an example
of such an infrastructure

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:25.820
that follows the
[INAUDIBLE] paradigm.

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:29.690
It allows you to run your
application and the tests

00:12:29.690 --> 00:12:32.540
either on prem or
on the public cloud.

00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:37.320
At Google, we also invest a lot
in smart test infrastructure.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:40.440
For example, machine
learning-based presubmits

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:43.950
that run fewer targeted tests
instead of the whole test

00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:46.590
suites based on
different signals,

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:49.590
especially from the change
list being submitted.

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:53.160
This will reduce the amount of
resources needed to run only

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:56.190
at the same time, keeping a
high confidence of our test

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:58.410
coverage and the signal.

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:01.230
Also, automatic
capacity planning,

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:04.080
where we could automatically
reduce the resources needed

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:09.110
per test run and reuse those
resources for other runs.

00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:12.710
We need to spend a lot of time
with our tests and our test

00:13:12.710 --> 00:13:14.360
infrastructure.

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:17.070
We start with
proper maintenance.

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:20.330
And here, I'm talking about
maintaining our critical user

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:24.950
journeys and tracking feature
coverage for our tests.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:28.190
To give you an example, the
GCE Live Migration feature

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:31.970
presented here is
mapped to certain CUJs

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:34.480
that we have tests to cover.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:37.960
Those tests are tracked and
we have automatic tooling

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:41.980
to track the coverage
based on the CUJs for GCE.

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:44.170
This helps us keep
focus on where testing

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:47.410
is needed the most, and
in preventing regressions

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:49.600
in CUJ test coverage.

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:52.690
We know that CUJ testing,
especially integration

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:54.880
end-to-end, are
very costly, and we

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:58.160
want to make the best
use of our testing.

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:00.700
We also need to keep an
eye on upgrading our test

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:03.490
infrastructure to
new technologies that

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:07.210
could allow us to run
tests faster or debug them

00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:09.310
or maintain them better.

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:12.220
At Google, we do this
by having core teams

00:14:12.220 --> 00:14:15.580
responsible of common test
infrastructure that is used

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:17.890
across different Google teams.

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:21.160
For example, we have a common
system integration test

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:23.650
framework that
allows us to benefit

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:26.170
from any improvements the
team does on that test

00:14:26.170 --> 00:14:28.810
infrastructure and
allow us to move quickly

00:14:28.810 --> 00:14:31.600
to new technologies.

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:35.410
We also want to look into adding
more test coverage and test

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:37.870
features as we see appropriate.

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:40.690
And here, we are
focusing on follow ups

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:44.260
on production outages, where
we do an analysis on what

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:46.840
testing is needed to prevent
those outages from happening

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:47.920
again.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:50.410
And also, based
on customer issues

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:52.990
or on test outages themselves.

00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:55.570
As I said, big
test outages could

00:14:55.570 --> 00:14:59.170
lead to more testing, different
pieces in the pipeline,

00:14:59.170 --> 00:15:02.170
especially moving left
through the development cycle.

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:04.630
Also, we need to look into
deleting and deprecating

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:05.920
our tests.

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:09.220
This is needed when
features are deprecated

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:12.760
or when critical user journeys'
priorities are changed.

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:15.070
And by that, we need to
keep an eye on that testing

00:15:15.070 --> 00:15:16.840
that we are running,
and deprecate them,

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:19.338
or change the flow we run them.

00:15:19.338 --> 00:15:21.130
CHAITALI NARLA: So
those were the five tips

00:15:21.130 --> 00:15:23.540
that we wanted to
share with you.

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:25.660
We have a couple of
key takeaways for you

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:27.960
from our talk.

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:30.830
The first one is
that mindful testing

00:15:30.830 --> 00:15:34.160
can help change your
organization's culture

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:38.750
to focus on healthy and
regularly cared for tests.

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:40.940
The second takeaway
we have for you

00:15:40.940 --> 00:15:43.430
is that mindful
testing will really

00:15:43.430 --> 00:15:48.640
help you get more return on
investment from your test.

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:54.810
We wanted to give a shout out to
two docs from Cloud Next 2019.

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:58.500
Both of these have a deep dive
into the different concepts

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:01.350
that we talked about
to date, and they also

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:05.660
talk about how you can apply
these concepts and thereby,

00:16:05.660 --> 00:16:10.050
mindful testing, by using
the different products we

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:12.900
have available on the
Google Cloud platform.

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:14.920
So do check them out.

00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:17.030
That's all we have to
share with you today.

00:16:17.030 --> 00:16:20.890
Thank you so much for
listening to our talk.

00:16:20.890 --> 00:16:22.890
SHADI KHALEK: Thank you
all for listening to us.

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:24.600
Please give us
feedback on how you

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:27.610
apply mindful testing
in your organization

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:30.370
and if you find
these tips useful.

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:32.060
Thank you.

